## Learning objectives

After this chapter, you should be able to trace conditional and loop-based C programs line by line, distinguish defined behavior from undefined behavior, reason about short-circuit evaluation, count loop iterations, and solve output-prediction questions commonly seen in GATE CSE.

## 1. Conditions and truth values in C

C does not require a condition to have the value `1` to mean true. In a scalar expression used as a condition:

- A value equal to zero means **false**.
- A value unequal to zero means **true**.

Examples:

```c
if (0)   puts("A");  /* body is skipped */
if (1)   puts("B");  /* body executes */
if (-10) puts("C");  /* body executes */
if (42)  puts("D");  /* body executes */
```

The last three conditions are true because their values are nonzero. This is different from saying that their values are all `1`.

### Logical results versus condition values

The logical operators `!`, `&&`, and `||` produce an `int` result equal to either `0` or `1`.

```c
int x = 17;
printf("%d %d\n", !!x, x);  /* prints: 1 17 */
```

`!!x` converts the truth value of `x` into a normalized Boolean-like value. It does not change `x`.

Using KaTeX, the condition rule is:

\[
\operatorname{truth}(x)=
\begin{cases}
0, & x=0\\
1, & x\ne 0
\end{cases}
\]

## 2. `if`, `if-else`, and nested conditions

### Basic `if`

```c
if (condition) {
    statement1;
    statement2;
}
```

The body executes only when the condition is nonzero. Braces are optional for a single statement, but using them consistently reduces errors.

### `if-else`

```c
if (marks >= 40) {
    puts("Pass");
} else {
    puts("Fail");
}
```

Exactly one branch is selected when the condition and program execution are well-defined.

### Nested `if` and the dangling-`else` rule

An `else` is associated with the **nearest preceding unmatched `if`**.

```c
if (a)
    if (b)
        puts("X");
    else
        puts("Y");
```

The `else` belongs to `if (b)`, not `if (a)`. Add braces whenever the intended association might be unclear:

```c
if (a) {
    if (b) {
        puts("X");
    } else {
        puts("Y");
    }
}
```

### Common trap: assignment is not comparison

```c
if (x = 5) {
    puts("yes");
}
```

`x = 5` assigns `5` to `x`. The assignment expression has value `5`, which is nonzero, so the body executes. This is valid C, though it is often a bug.

```c
if (x == 5) {
    puts("equal");
}
```

`==` tests equality; `=` assigns. Do not automatically call the first form a syntax error.

## 3. Relational and logical operators

Relational operators are `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, `==`, and `!=`. Their results are `0` or `1`.

Logical operators:

| Operator | Meaning | Result is true when |
|---|---|---|
| `!A` | Logical NOT | `A` is false |
| `A && B` | Logical AND | both operands are true |
| `A || B` | Logical OR | at least one operand is true |

Example:

```c
int a = 4, b = 0;
printf("%d\n", a > 2 && b != 0);  /* 0 */
printf("%d\n", a > 2 || b != 0);  /* 1 */
printf("%d\n", !b);               /* 1 */
```

### Logical versus bitwise operators

Do not confuse these pairs:

- `&&` versus `&`
- `||` versus `|`
- `!` versus `~`

Logical operators reason about truth values and return `0` or `1`. Bitwise operators operate on integer bits. In particular, `&&` and `||` short-circuit; bitwise `&` and `|` do not.

## 4. Short-circuit evaluation

C guarantees left-to-right evaluation of the operands of `&&` and `||`, with a sequence point between the first operand and the second operand.

- `A && B`: if `A` is false, `B` is not evaluated.
- `A || B`: if `A` is true, `B` is not evaluated.

```c
if (x != 0 && y / x > 2) {
    puts("condition holds");
}
```

If `x == 0`, the left operand `x != 0` is false. The right operand `y / x > 2` is skipped, so this expression avoids division by zero in that right operand.

Another example:

```c
int i = 0;
int result = (i != 0) && (10 / i > 1);
/* result is 0; 10 / i is not evaluated */
```

For OR:

```c
int i = 5;
int result = (i == 5) || (++i > 10);
/* result is 1; ++i is not evaluated, so i remains 5 */
```

### Short-circuit trace

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["Evaluate left operand A"] --> B{"Which operator?"}
    B -->|AND| C{"Is A true?"}
    C -->|No| D["Result is 0; skip B"]
    C -->|Yes| E["Evaluate B; result is B's truth value"]
    B -->|OR| F{"Is A true?"}
    F -->|Yes| G["Result is 1; skip B"]
    F -->|No| H["Evaluate B; result is B's truth value"]
```

The diagram summarizes the short-circuit rule. For a concrete expression, identify which operator is being used (`&&` or `||`) before tracing the right operand.

## 5. The conditional operator `?:`

Syntax:

```c
condition ? expression_if_true : expression_if_false
```

Only one of the second and third operands is evaluated.

```c
int x = 0;
int y = x ? 10 : 20;  /* y becomes 20 */
```

Side-effect example:

```c
int i = 3;
int x = (i > 0) ? i++ : i--;
/* x becomes 3; i becomes 4 */
```

Because `i > 0` is true, `i++` is selected and `i--` is not evaluated. The conditional operator is an expression; `if-else` is a statement.

## 6. `switch`, `case`, `break`, and fall-through

A `switch` selects a matching case label based on an integer-valued controlling expression after the language's required integer promotions.

```c
switch (choice) {
    case 1:
        puts("one");
        break;
    case 2:
        puts("two");
        break;
    default:
        puts("other");
}
```

Important rules:

1. `case` labels must be integer constant expressions.
2. Case values within the same `switch` must be distinct after the relevant conversions.
3. `default` is optional. If no case matches and `default` exists, execution begins there.
4. `break` exits the nearest enclosing loop or `switch`; it is not inserted automatically.
5. Without `break`, execution continues into following statements and case labels. This is called **fall-through**.
6. A `switch` does not directly accept a string as its controlling expression in C.

Example of fall-through:

```c
int x = 1;
switch (x) {
    case 1: printf("A");
    case 2: printf("B"); break;
    default: printf("C");
}
/* Output: AB */
```

Execution starts at `case 1`, prints `A`, then falls through and prints `B`. The `break` then exits the `switch`.

A `break` inside a `switch` nested in a loop exits the `switch`, not the surrounding loop. A subsequent statement in the loop can still execute.

### 6.1 `default` in the middle, one `default`, and valid case labels

Matching and execution are separate steps:

1. The controlling expression is compared with every `case` label. If one matches, execution begins at that label.
2. Only if **no** label matches does execution begin at `default`, wherever `default` is written.
3. From the starting point, execution continues **downward in source order**, through any later labels, until a `break` or the closing brace.

Therefore a `default` placed before other cases still falls through into the cases written after it:

```c
int x = -4;
switch (x) {
    case 0:  x = x + 1; break;
    default: x = x - 1;       /* entered: no label matches -4 */
    case 1:  x = x - 11;      /* fall-through */
    case 2:  x = x + 128;     /* fall-through */
             break;
}
/* x: -4 -> -5 -> -16 -> 112, and printf("%c", x) prints 'p' */
```

If a label does match, `default` is skipped unless execution falls into it:

```c
switch (2) {
    case 1:  printf("A");
    default: printf("D");
    case 2:  printf("B");     /* starts here: prints B, then C */
    case 3:  printf("C");
}
/* Output: BC */
```

Rules and traps:

- A `switch` may contain **at most one** `default`.
- `case` labels must be **integer constant expressions** (integers, characters, enumerators, `sizeof`, constant arithmetic such as `2+3`). `case 1.5:` and a `case` with a variable are errors.
- The controlling expression must have integer type; a `switch` on a `float` or `double` is an error.
- Two case labels with the same value in one `switch` are an error.
- The order of case labels does not affect which one matches; it affects only fall-through.

## 7. The `for` loop

General syntax:

```c
for (initialization; condition; update) {
    body;
}
```

The normal execution order is:

1. Evaluate initialization once.
2. Evaluate the condition.
3. If false, exit the loop.
4. Execute the body.
5. Execute the update expression.
6. Return to the condition.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["Initialization (once)"] --> B["Test condition"]
    B -->|False| E["Exit loop"]
    B -->|True| C["Execute body"]
    C --> D["Execute update"]
    D --> B
```

Example:

```c
int i;
for (i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
    printf("%d ", i);
}
/* Output: 0 1 2 3 */
```

The body runs for `i = 0, 1, 2, 3`. When `i` becomes `4`, the condition is false.

### Missing expressions in a `for` loop

The three control expressions are syntactically optional:

```c
for (;;) {
    /* no condition means the loop has no normal condition-based exit */
    break;
}
```

`for (; condition; )` is legal. An omitted condition behaves as a nonzero condition. Such a loop may still terminate through `break`, `return`, or another control transfer.

### `continue` in a `for` loop

A `continue` skips the remaining body and proceeds to the loop's update expression, then the condition is tested again.

```c
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    if (i == 2)
        continue;
    printf("%d ", i);
}
/* Output: 0 1 3 4 */
```

The update `i++` still occurs after `continue`, so this loop progresses normally.

## 8. The `while` loop

```c
while (condition) {
    body;
}
```

The condition is tested before each execution of the body. Therefore, a `while` loop can execute zero times.

```c
int i = 5;
while (i < 3) {
    printf("%d", i);
    i++;
}
/* Prints nothing */
```

A common pattern is to update the loop variable in the body:

```c
int i = 0;
while (i < 3) {
    printf("%d ", i);
    i++;
}
/* Output: 0 1 2 */
```

If no statement can make the condition false and no control transfer exits the loop, the loop may continue indefinitely.

## 9. The `do-while` loop

```c
do {
    body;
} while (condition);
```

The body executes before the condition is checked, so it executes at least once (assuming control reaches the loop and the body itself does not transfer control away).

```c
int i = 5;
do {
    printf("%d ", i);
    i++;
} while (i < 3);
/* Output: 5 */
```

The semicolon after `while (condition)` is required. The key comparison is:

| Loop | When condition is tested | Minimum body executions |
|---|---|---:|
| `for` | Before each iteration | 0 |
| `while` | Before each iteration | 0 |
| `do-while` | After each iteration | 1 |

## 10. `break` and `continue`

### `break`

`break` terminates the nearest enclosing loop or `switch`.

```c
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    if (i == 3)
        break;
    printf("%d ", i);
}
/* Output: 0 1 2 */
```

### `continue`

`continue` skips the remainder of the current iteration of the nearest enclosing loop.

```c
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    if (i == 3)
        continue;
    printf("%d ", i);
}
/* Output: 0 1 2 4 */
```

In a `while` loop, `continue` jumps to the condition check. Be careful: if the increment is placed later in the body, a `continue` before that increment may prevent progress and create an infinite loop.

```c
int i = 0;
while (i < 5) {
    if (i == 2) {
        i++;       /* update before continue */
        continue;
    }
    printf("%d ", i);
    i++;
}
```

This prints `0 1 3 4`. If `i++` were omitted from the `if` branch, the loop would remain at `i == 2` forever.

### Nested loops and `break`

An unlabelled `break` exits only the nearest loop or `switch`, not every nested loop.

```c
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
        if (j == 1)
            break;
        printf("(%d,%d) ", i, j);
    }
}
/* Prints (0,0) (1,0) (2,0) */
```

## 11. Loop tracing: use a table

For output questions, track variables after every meaningful step rather than guessing.

Example:

```c
int i = 0, s = 0;
while (i < 4) {
    i++;
    if (i == 2)
        continue;
    s += i;
}
printf("%d", s);
```

| Iteration | `i` after increment | `i == 2`? | Update to `s` | `s` afterward |
|---:|---:|---|---|---:|
| 1 | 1 | No | `s += 1` | 1 |
| 2 | 2 | Yes | Skipped | 1 |
| 3 | 3 | No | `s += 3` | 4 |
| 4 | 4 | No | `s += 4` | 8 |

Output: `8`.

For each iteration, record:
- the condition value;
- the variables before and after the body;
- whether `break` or `continue` changes the normal flow;
- the update expression, if the loop is a `for` loop.

## 12. Counting loop iterations and time complexity

If an outer loop executes \(n\) times and, for every outer iteration, an inner loop also executes \(n\) times, the inner statement executes \(n^2\) times.

```c
for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
    for (j = 0; j < n; j++)
        count++;
```

The exact count is:

\[
\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}1=n^2
\]

But dependent bounds change the count:

```c
for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
    for (j = 0; j < i; j++)
        count++;
```

The exact count is:

\[
\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}i=\frac{n(n-1)}{2}
\]

Thus the running time is \(\Theta(n^2)\), for positive growing \(n\).

For a loop that doubles a variable each iteration:

```c
for (i = 1; i < n; i *= 2) {
    /* constant-time body */
}
```

the number of iterations is \(\Theta(\log n)\), provided the values and types used do not overflow and the loop condition behaves as intended.

For nested loops, never decide complexity only by counting the number of `for` keywords. Derive the iteration count from the actual bounds and updates.

## 13. C-specific traps and defined behavior

### Do not assume left-to-right evaluation everywhere

`&&` and `||` have guaranteed short-circuit sequencing, but this does **not** mean every operator evaluates its operands left to right. For example, the operand evaluation order of `+` is not generally specified as left-to-right.

Avoid code that modifies the same scalar object multiple times without the sequencing required by C. Such code may have undefined behavior; if behavior is undefined, do not try to calculate a single output.

### Avoid accidental signed overflow in loop updates

For a signed integer, an update that overflows its representable range has undefined behavior. For example, do not rely on a signed `int` eventually wrapping around to become negative. Unsigned arithmetic wraps modulo one more than the maximum representable value, but that does not make every unsigned loop correct.

### Do not change a loop variable unexpectedly

If a loop body modifies its own control variable, trace that modification together with the loop update. The number of iterations may differ from the apparent count.

### Empty statement trap

A semicolon immediately after a loop header is an empty body:

```c
for (i = 0; i < 3; i++);  /* loop body is empty */
printf("%d", i);          /* prints 3 */
```

Indentation does not change C syntax. Always identify exactly which statement belongs to the loop.

## 14. GATE CSE code-tracing procedure

Use this checklist for each problem:

1. Identify the initial values and declared types.
2. Add braces mentally to clarify which statements belong to each `if`, loop, or `else`.
3. For each condition, evaluate zero/nonzero truth correctly.
4. For `&&` and `||`, determine whether the right operand is skipped.
5. For `switch`, start at the matching case and trace fall-through until a `break` or the end.
6. For a loop, write the exact sequence: initialization, condition, body, update.
7. Handle `continue` according to the loop type; handle `break` only for the nearest loop or `switch`.
8. Count iterations from actual bounds, especially when bounds depend on another variable.
9. Check for invalid memory access, division by zero, signed overflow, or unsequenced side effects.
10. Only report an output if the program's behavior is defined and the output is determined.

## 15. Quick revision sheet

- `0` is false; every nonzero scalar condition is true.
- `!!x` is `0` or `1`.
- `=` assigns; `==` compares.
- `&&` and `||` short-circuit.
- The conditional operator `?:` evaluates only the selected operand.
- `else` associates with the nearest unmatched `if`.
- `switch` can fall through without `break`.
- `for` runs initialization once; `continue` reaches its update expression.
- `while` can execute zero times; `do-while` executes its body at least once.
- `break` exits the nearest loop or `switch`.
- `continue` skips the rest of the current iteration.
- A `continue` in a `while` loop can accidentally skip the variable update.
- An empty statement after a loop header can make the loop body empty.
- For two independent \(n\)-iteration loops, the inner statement executes \(n^2\) times.
- For `j < i` with `i = 0` to `n - 1`, the count is \(n(n-1)/2\).
- Do not calculate an output for code with undefined behavior.
- A `switch` has at most one `default`; it runs only when no label matches, but wherever it is written it falls through into the labels written after it.

## 1. Dynamic allocation

Include `<stdlib.h>` for `malloc`, `calloc`, `realloc`, and `free`.

- `malloc(size)`: allocates `size` bytes; contents are uninitialized.
- `calloc(count, size)`: allocates space for an array and sets all allocated bytes to zero. Zeroed bytes should not be generalized to every possible object's semantic zero representation.
- `realloc(ptr, new_size)`: changes allocation size and may move the block.
- `free(ptr)`: releases allocated storage; `free(NULL)` is safe.

Always check allocation results for `NULL` before using them.

## 2. Safer `realloc` pattern

```c
int *temp = realloc(p, new_count * sizeof *p);
if (temp != NULL) {
    p = temp;
} else {
    /* p remains valid if realloc failed for a nonzero requested size */
}
```

Assigning `realloc` directly to the only pointer can lose the original allocation on failure. For a nonzero requested size, on failure the original allocation remains valid. Zero-size `realloc` has special/version-sensitive rules, so avoid relying on it for portable cleanup.

After successful `realloc`, if the allocation moved, old aliases to the prior block must not be used. After `free(p)`, do not dereference the old pointer; assigning `p = NULL` can help prevent accidental reuse, but does not nullify other aliases.

## 3. Dynamic arrays

```c
int *a = malloc(n * sizeof *a);
if (a != NULL) {
    a[i] = 10;
    free(a);
}
```

Using `sizeof *a` ties the requested element size to the pointed-to type. Check multiplication for size overflow in robust production code.

For a contiguous dynamically allocated 2D array with `m` columns:

```c
int (*a)[m] = malloc(n * sizeof *a);
if (a != NULL) {
    a[i][j] = 7;
    free(a);
}
```

This is a pointer to rows of `m` integers. An alternative is an array of pointers with separately allocated rows; that representation may not be contiguous and needs different cleanup.

## 4. Leaks, wild pointers, and dangling pointers

A memory leak occurs when allocated storage is no longer reachable and cannot be released by the program. A wild pointer is uninitialized. A dangling pointer refers to an object whose lifetime ended, including storage released by `free`. Initialize pointers, free allocations exactly as required, and avoid double-free and use-after-free.

## 5. Conceptual process memory

A process is commonly described as having code/text, read-only data, global/static storage, heap, and stack regions. This is a conceptual model, not a universal C guarantee about exact layout. The heap commonly serves dynamic allocation; stack frames commonly hold function-call state and automatic locals.

A recursive call creates another active invocation and usually another stack frame. File-scope and static local variables have static storage duration. An uninitialized file-scope `int` is zero-initialized, unlike an uninitialized automatic local `int`, whose value is indeterminate.
